<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">relics</ansi>

Relics are hand-made gear that bosses carry: <ansi fg="rarity-legendary">Legendaries</ansi> and <ansi fg="rarity-set">Sets</ansi>. They are tier 4
to 6 (<ansi fg="command">help equipmenttiers</ansi>), they have a name of their own, and they do
something no ordinary piece does. Ordinary foes never drop them.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Legendary</ansi>

A named item with one fixed <ansi fg="yellow">signature</ansi> that works while it is worn, such as
Ashen Reaper's Reaping (blows deal 20% more to a foe at or below half
health) or the Grave-Pact Circlet's Grave Pact (once a battle a felling blow
leaves its wearer at 1 health). The signature is known on sight: <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> at
the item and it says what it does. A Legendary also rolls two ordinary
affixes, which stay hidden until a Scribe reads it (<ansi fg="command">help identify</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Set</ansi>

A small set, three pieces, from one boss. A piece alone is plain armor.
Wear two pieces of one set and the set gives its first bonus; wear all three
and it gives the second as well. A piece worn twice counts once. <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> at a
piece to see its set and both bonuses.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What the effects are</ansi>

Signatures and set bonuses are the same effects a class has: more Attack or
Evasion, a bonus on every blow, more damage to a wounded foe, a second wind,
protection for the allies in the wearer's row, and so on. They add to what
the wearer's class already gives and to the other relics worn, and they
work for a companion exactly as for you. Every one is capped, so a relic is
a strong choice, not a runaway one. They end the moment the piece comes off.
<ansi fg="command">inventory</ansi> lists the relics you wear, each signature in words, and each set's
progress with the bonuses in force.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where they come from</ansi>

Every relic belongs to one boss, and a Scribe of rank 4 can read which. Each
company that beats the boss rolls once a kill for at most one of its relics.
Each relic has a few percent, so a boss that carries several drops one more
often: from about one kill in twelve to one in four. Allied companies each roll
for themselves.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Bad luck</ansi>

A boss you keep beating without a relic owes you one: the 20th kill of the
same boss since your last relic from it is certain to drop one. The count is
kept for you across restarts, one for each boss, and starts over when a
relic drops.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Wearing and selling</ansi>

A relic has a fixed item level, and needs a character 5 levels below it
(<ansi fg="command">help itemlevel</ansi>). Its numbers are the item's own, whatever the quality of
the day: relics do not roll quality. Hand one to a companion with
<ansi fg="command">company equip</ansi>, or sell it: a merchant pays a quarter of its value at
most, which is still the best price any find fetches (<ansi fg="command">help sell</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help rarity</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help equipmenttiers</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help loot</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help encounters</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help identify</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help equipment</ansi>.
